cartridge "LEXITRON/RON WILSON/1986"

' LEXITRON
' A ColecoVision game based on "Lexitron" by Ron Wilson, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette February 1986 (Commodore 64 and 128, BASIC; disk 1986-02).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a cell-for-cell port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' C64 listing, orig/lexitron.bas; the 128 version is the same program),
' the game is built for the ColecoVision: a smooth sliding cursor, tiled
' letters that light up when found, a framed field with two energy tubes,
' sound effects and an original title tune.
'
' The game: ten words hide in a field of 20 x 15 letters. Move the cursor
' over a letter of a hidden word and press the button: the letter lights
' up and your energy rises by one. Light every letter of a word to find
' it; find all ten to win (your time is shown). Pressing on a letter that
' belongs to no word costs one energy, and the energy also drains at
' random moments. Sometimes a clue names a word you have not started.
' When the energy runs out the game is over and the words you missed are
' shown in red.
'
' From the original: the 20 x 15 field, ten words from the list (the
' original's twelve words are all here), the levels (1: words read
' forwards, across or down; 2: also backwards; 3: also diagonally), no
' two words crossing, random letters elsewhere; a press on a word letter
' lights it and gives one energy (up to 15), a press on any other letter
' costs one ("TRY ANOTHER"), a press on a lit letter does nothing; the
' energy starts at 7 and the game ends on a drain at 0; random drains at
' 3 chances in 1024 a pass and clues at 1 in 1024 (the original's .009 and
' .003 per loop, the same 3 to 1 ratio), a clue only for a word with no
' letter found; messages GOOD ANSWER, TRY ANOTHER, WELL DONE!, CLUE:; the
' time shown at a win; the missed words shown at a loss.
'
' Liberties: a list of 64 words instead of 12 (the original picked ten
' consecutive words from its shuffled twelve, so every game hid nearly the
' same words); the cursor slides a cell in 8 frames, diagonals allowed as
' on the C64; at the field's edge a diagonal keeps its free axis; a press
' made while the cursor slides is kept for its arrival; a clue stays on
' screen 2.5 seconds while play goes on (the C64 stopped for about one);
' the original's chance of 1/1024 a pass is measured per video frame
' (60 a second; the BASIC loop ran perhaps 20 times a second, so drains and
' clues come a little more often: mean 5.7 and 17 seconds); words may start
' in the field's first row and column (the original's INT(RND*19+1) never
' chose them); the time and the words found are shown during play; the
' level menu is on the title. The title tune and sounds are new.
'
' Controls: either controller; the stick moves the cursor, a side button
' marks the letter under it. On the title, up and down choose a level,
' HOW TO PLAY or CREDITS, a button starts or shows the page (the cursor
' comes back on the last level played).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given. Own
' xorshift random numbers. Nothing flashes.

